Revision history for Unblock-HTTP3

0.03  2026-10-04
    - Require Uniform::HTTP 0.06 for its header-only native FastPath ABI.
    - Inspect canonical outgoing Request and Response objects directly in XS,
      avoiding Perl FastPath views and temporary HTTP/3 wire-field arrays.
    - Retain decoded nghttp3 header blocks in native connection state and build
      exact canonical Uniform messages directly from validated native byte spans.
    - Preserve HTTP/3 semantic validation, Cookie coalescing, field-section
      limits, message lifecycle state, and canonical Uniform object ownership.
    - Add native-path loopback coverage and HTTP-layer microbenchmarks.
    - Add a public versioned native consumer ABI for XS event frameworks and
      HTTP libraries, with an installed C header and persistent Connection
      context.
    - Expose native service, request, Transaction polling, canonical Uniform
      message access, response sending, and failure-state inspection without
      exposing libnghttp3 or private storage.

0.02  2026-10-04
    - Require Uniform::HTTP 0.05 and use exact canonical Uniform::HTTP::Request
      and Uniform::HTTP::Response objects throughout the public HTTP message API.
    - Remove the Unblock::HTTP3 Request and Response subclasses; HTTP/3
      priority and stream-abort state now belong to Transaction.
    - Use Uniform::HTTP::FastPath ABI 1 for outgoing wire-field access and
      trusted construction of already-validated received HTTP/3 messages.
    - Record local and remote RESET_STREAM and STOP_SENDING codes explicitly
      on Transaction without adding transport state to Uniform message objects.
    - Add RFC 9412 ORIGIN support through libnghttp3.
    - Add server origins configuration and client peer_origins inspection.
    - Add independent quic-go client interoperability against an
      Unblock::HTTP3 server.
    - Expand interoperability documentation to cover both client and server
      directions.
    - Add repeatable real UDP/TLS/QUIC/HTTP/3 loopback performance benchmarks.
    - Add split-process benchmarks to separate client/server event-driving cost.
    - Add HTTP/3-layer microbenchmarks for Uniform message construction,
      wire-field preparation, field-section accounting, native nghttp3
      submission, and streaming body-copy cost.
    - Avoid rebuilding the connection-specific header lookup for every field,
      reducing representative HTTP/3 wire-field preparation cost.
    - Retain outgoing body chunks as dedicated Perl scalar storage in native
      state instead of allocating and copying a second C byte buffer, while
      preserving caller-mutation isolation and ACK-driven release.
    - Add incoming-body microbenchmarks for native DATA events, buffered
      accumulation, streaming delivery, and complete buffered construction.
    - Retain owned incoming DATA scalars directly in streaming Body::Reader,
      removing a second Perl string copy while preserving receive-credit and
      backpressure semantics.
    - Keep small buffered DATA chunks on the compact scalar path and retain
      owned chunks of 16 KiB or larger until one final message-body coalescing,
      reducing repeated large-body copies.
    - Add a normative RFC conformance audit suite for malformed control,
      QPACK, priority, push, and stream-lifecycle input.
    - Treat peer closure, reset, or STOP_SENDING of critical HTTP/3 streams
      as H3_CLOSED_CRITICAL_STREAM.
    - Reject duplicate peer SETTINGS with H3_SETTINGS_ERROR so extension and
      remembered 0-RTT state cannot become ambiguous.
    - Reject unauthorized server push with H3_ID_ERROR when no push capacity
      has been advertised.
    - Coalesce decompressed Cookie field lines before exposing canonical
      Uniform HTTP messages.
    - Defer server application delivery of 0-RTT Transactions and Datagrams
      until the QUIC handshake completes and the early data has not been
      rejected.
    - Enforce the 0-or-1 value rules for Extended CONNECT and HTTP Datagram
      SETTINGS in the HTTP/3 SETTINGS inspector.
    - Enforce the RFC 9297 Capsule-Protocol response-status restriction.
    - Document the remaining libnghttp3 limitations for malformed-message
      stream scope and RFC 9218 invalid-parameter preservation.

0.01  2026-10-04
    - Start Unblock::HTTP3.
    - Require released Net::QUIC 0.04 for QUIC transport and RFC 9221 DATAGRAM.
    - Use Alien::nghttp3 0.01 for libnghttp3.
    - Add native client and server libnghttp3 connection state.
    - Add the HTTP/3 control and QPACK stream bridge.
    - Use Uniform::HTTP 0.04 as the runtime HTTP message layer.
    - Make Unblock::HTTP3::Request and Unblock::HTTP3::Response thin subclasses
      of the canonical Uniform request and response classes.
    - Accept plain Uniform::HTTP::Request objects directly for client requests.
    - Use Uniform's Extended CONNECT protocol metadata, ordered trailers,
      section mutability, fidelity, and completeness APIs directly.
    - Move streaming-body and buffered receive progress into Transaction so a
      Uniform body is exposed only when the complete buffered body is known.
    - Remove the duplicate Unblock::HTTP3::_Message implementation.
    - Add Unblock::HTTP3::Transaction for multiplexed request/response state.
    - Add real request and response exchange over UDP, QUIC, and TLS.
    - Add buffered request and response bodies.
    - Add incremental outgoing bodies with ACK-driven backpressure.
    - Add streaming incoming bodies with explicit QUIC receive credit.
    - Add request and response trailers.
    - Reject framing and routing fields such as Content-Length, Host, and TE
      from outgoing trailers.
    - Add 1xx informational responses.
    - Add basic CONNECT tunnels with bidirectional DATA and half-close.
    - Add generic Extended CONNECT negotiation and :protocol support.
    - Add generic RFC 9297 Capsule framing, parsing, dispatch, and streams.
    - Add RFC 9297 HTTP Datagrams over Net::QUIC 0.04, including
      SETTINGS_H3_DATAGRAM, Quarter Stream ID routing, Transaction send/receive
      APIs, bounded receive buffering, and H3_DATAGRAM_ERROR handling.
    - Add persistent opaque HTTP/3 SETTINGS state for 0-RTT.
    - Add explicit replay-aware 0-RTT requests and HTTP Datagrams.
    - Validate accepted 0-RTT SETTINGS, including RFC 9204 QPACK rules.
    - Roll rejected QUIC 0-RTT back to fresh HTTP/3 state for safe 1-RTT retry.
    - Add generic HTTP/3 extension unidirectional streams.
    - Add RFC 9218 request priority fields and live Transaction priority updates.
    - Synchronize libnghttp3 client-stream ID validation with Net::QUIC stream limits.
    - Add rejected CONNECT handling and CONNECT cleanup coverage.
    - Add out-of-order multiplexing coverage.
    - Add STOP_SENDING, RESET_STREAM, and stream-close handling.
    - Release cancelled streaming buffers while preserving late ACK
      accounting.
    - Add bounded transmit and receive buffering.
    - Add field-section and body resource limits.
    - Honor peer SETTINGS_MAX_FIELD_SECTION_SIZE before sending header or
      trailer sections.
    - Add configurable QPACK limits.
    - Validate HTTP/3 62-bit integer settings before native setup.
    - Add generic extension SETTINGS advertisement, peer inspection, and validation.
    - Respect HTTP/3-reserved and GREASE setting identifiers.
    - Add HTTP/3 field validation and connection-specific field rules.
    - Validate Host/:authority routing, http/https path form, and CONNECT
      host/port semantics.
    - Reject malformed received routing fields with H3_MESSAGE_ERROR on the
      affected request stream without closing unrelated streams.
    - Add bodyless response rules for HEAD, 204, 205, and 304.
    - Validate outgoing Content-Length for buffered and streaming bodies.
    - Add HTTP/3 graceful shutdown support.
    - Map fatal libnghttp3 parser errors to QUIC application close errors.
    - Remove completed stream, transaction, and native body state.
    - Keep event loops, UDP sockets, TLS, and QUIC packet processing
      outside Unblock::HTTP3.
    - Test dependencies from CPAN rather than GitHub development code.
    - Make TLS loopback tests self-contained with bundled localhost fixtures
      instead of requiring the openssl command-line tool.
    - Simplify README and public POD into the standard practical CPAN format.
    - Add consistent SYNOPSIS, METHODS, SEE ALSO, AUTHOR, and LICENSE sections
      across the public API documentation.
    - Keep repository-only planning, release, contribution, security, and
      public-network test material out of the CPAN distribution.
    - Add public interoperability tests against Cloudflare and LiteSpeed HTTP/3
      servers, with Google retained as a diagnostic target.
